A||||5||Ironhorse||Prep for Autocross||||6||
Z||000000||Ironhorse||05-29-2000||07:12 PM||||hey ya'll I got a pal whose got a 1985 sundamaged/faded black 4cyc/5sp Fiero.  He wants to go to autocross.  He want to go into stock division and would like to know what he can do to imporve cornering, and putting extra weight in the front.  Gimme ALLLLL the information about prep for autocross and etc. I'll pass the information along to him.  Oh his car is completely bone stock, nothing modified.  Thanks for any info.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Phaeton||05-30-2000||01:08 AM||||Bone stock?  Empty both trunks and overinflate the tires.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Greg Piet||05-30-2000||11:28 AM||||Overinflate the tires?<BR>I run at Yoko S306 tires in my 85 SE 6 banger, with the fronts at 29 and the rears at 28.  Overinflating the tires will not allow you to use the entire patch of the tires.  There are little arrows on the side of the tires.  Those tell you how far you can ride on the sidewall.  After a few runs the tires will also heat up significantly especially with a rookie squealing the tires(from 30 to 48 psi has happened on my Yoko's).  Putting some chalk on the sidewalls will allow you to judge how much side roll you are getting and adjust tire pressure accordingly. <A HREF="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Fiero1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Fiero1.html</A> <BR>has a pic of my Fiero on an autocross and you an see the chaulk marks im referring to. <P>As far as removing all the weight in the front that may be good for acceleration, but the fiero is weight deprived in the front which reduces steering .  Since all the weight is in the back, late breaking is required to shift as much weight as possible to the front (steering) tires, since the amount steering possible is directly proportional to the weight on the wheels.  The spare in the front adds weight to the front tires and the late breaking shifts weight to the front tires to improve the steering.    <P>Not that I am an expert racecar driver, but I must disagree with Phaeton, just due to the physics involved, especially since autocrossing is not high speed, and a stock 4 cylinder shouldnt notice whether you cut 30 pounds off of the front as far as a 0-30 time.  The driver skill is much more a factor than whether every possible ounce has been removed from the car.  This si even more so is ES (I think the 2.5 is is E class).<P>Here is a site that lists other peoples setups for Fieros and other cars for autocross events <A HREF="http://www.autocross.com/autoc/setups/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autocross.com/autoc/setups/index.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||05-30-2000||10:59 PM||||I always overinflated my non performance tires to keep the sidewalls up off the surface.  The Fiero felt more solid to my ass-o-meter, but we all know how the meter stands up to a stopwatch.<BR>I read the forum every day just because of practices like this that I just feel are right but aren't.  I'm going to go out and lower the pressure in my tires,  44 psi all around seems like a lot now.<P>||||reg||12
Z||000004||Greg Piet||05-31-2000||12:23 PM||||This is also being discussed in the general section.   SCCA Fiero seems to agree with Phaeton. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002379.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002379.html</A> <P>I know if I ran at those tire pressures I would be all over the course, and I wouldnt be using the entire tire.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||06-01-2000||02:38 AM||||The higher pressures will make the Fiero break loose easier, but with my skill level that is good.<BR>Race tires set up for max traction held on so long I'd tense up waiting for them to break traction, then when traction came back it came back with a vengeance and snapped me back the other way.<BR>Things got better, but that was months down the road 'cause I was used to dirt tracks and a very loose rear end.<P>Now I just LIKE driving sideways!!  Slowly in the winter and fastly in the summer.  ||||reg||1
